.TH NOTIFICATION 1 "October 2026" "monowl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
notification \- send and manage monopanel bar notifications
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBnotification send\fR [\fItext\fR]
\fBnotification question\fR [\fB--timeout\fR \fIN\fR] [\fByesorno\fR|\fBanswers\fR] \fIquestion\fR [\fIanswer\fR ...]
\fBnotification list\fR
\fBnotification delete\fR \fIindex-or-id\fR
\fBnotification deleteall\fR
\fBnotification --help\fR
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B notification
manages the notification queue shown in the monopanel top bar.
The bar shows ONE notification icon, clock-adjacent (rightmost, left of
the date), always drawn: a blank/dim pill when the queue is empty, an
amber count pill showing TOTAL waiting (plain messages plus questions)
when anything waits. No message text ever renders on the bar.
.PP
Clicking the icon (a no-op when the count is zero) opens a tiny
layer-shell preview popup listing up to 3 oldest plain rows (first
lines, FIFO) plus at most 1 question row (oldest question). Clicking a
plain row dismisses that one entry and the count drops; clicking the
question row spawns
.B notification-gui ,
the centered layer-shell answer deck, and closes the preview. The
preview closes on selection, on an empty queue, on pointer-leave, or on
Esc.
.PP
The answer deck shows one button per answer, oldest question first,
auto-advancing to the next question and auto-closing when the queue
drains. Because it is layer-shell and not a client, the deck survives
Super+D (show-desktop hides clients, never layer surfaces). Esc
dismisses the current question without answering. Questions are never
dismissible as plain rows.
.PP
The queue is files in
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/monowl/notifications/ ,
one file per entry, so any process that can write there can post.
The panel picks up new entries on its 1Hz tick. Plain messages are
.B .txt
files; questions are
.B .q
files: line 1 is the question text (capped at 512 bytes), lines 2 and
on carry the custom answers, one per line (1 to 10 lines for the
.B answers
type; a single-line file is the classic Yes/No). Answering writes a
.B .answer
rendezvous file holding the selected answer text verbatim (lowercase
"yes"/"no" in the file for the yesorno type, printed capitalized Yes/No
on stdout); the waiting
.B question
caller polls for it every 0.5s.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "send " text
Queue one message. Arguments are joined with spaces; newlines become
spaces and the text is capped at 512 bytes (preview rows display the
first 160 characters). With no
arguments, the message is read from stdin.
.TP
.B question [--timeout N] [yesorno|answers] question [answer ...]
Ask a question and block until it is answered, printing the answer to
stdout. Three forms:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Legacy:
.B "question \(dqship it?\(dq"
asks Yes/No (same as spelling out
.BR yesorno ).
With no text argument the question is read from stdin.
.IP \(bu 2
Explicit Yes/No:
.B "question yesorno \(dqship it?\(dq"
takes no answers; extra arguments are an error.
.IP \(bu 2
Custom answers:
.B "question answers \(dqpick one\(dq \(dqred\(dq \(dqgreen\(dq \(dqblue\(dq"
requires 1 to 10 non-empty answers (fewer or more is an error); each
answer is capped at 160 characters.
.RE
.RS
.B --timeout
bounds the wait in seconds (0, the default, waits forever). On timeout, on manual
.B delete
of the question, or on interrupt the queue entry is removed and it
exits 2. Progress and warning notices (waiting-for-answer status,
\(dqpanel not running\(dq, \(dqtimed out\(dq, \(dqdismissed unread\(dq)
go to stderr, so stdout stays exactly the
answer for
.B $(...)
use.
.RE
.TP
.B list
Show the queue oldest-first, one per line, as
.IR INDEX " ID " TEXT .
Index 1 is the oldest entry in the queue. Questions carry a
.B [yes/no?]
suffix, or
.BI [answers " N]"
with the number of custom answers.
.TP
.BI "delete " index-or-id
Remove one entry: by list index (1 = oldest) or by id prefix copied
from
.BR list .
Purely numeric arguments inside the list range are treated as indexes;
anything else is matched as an id prefix and must match exactly one
file (either a plain message or a question).
.TP
.B deleteall
Clear the whole queue (plain messages and questions).
.TP
.BR --help ", " -h ", " help
Show usage on stdout and exit 0.
.PP
Running
.B notification
with no command, or with an unknown command or wrong argument count,
prints usage to stderr and exits 1.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.B question :
answered (prints Yes or the chosen answer).
.TP
.B 1
.B question
with the yesorno type: the answer was No (prints No).
Also used for usage errors.
.TP
.B 2
.B question :
timeout, dismiss (deleted unread, or Esc in the GUI), or interrupt.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
.RS
notification send "build done"
echo "tests green" | notification send
# legacy Yes/No question (exit 0 = Yes, 1 = No):
if notification question "ship it?"; then echo "user said Yes"; fi
# explicit Yes/No:
notification question yesorno "ship it?"
# custom answers (prints exactly the picked answer):
notification question answers "pick one" "red" "green" "blue"
# bounded wait (exit 2 on timeout):
notification question --timeout 60 "still there?"
notification question --timeout 30 answers "pick one" "a" "b"
notification list
notification delete 1
notification deleteall
# Super+D flow: press Super+D to clear to the desktop, then click
# the notification icon; the preview popup lists the waiting items
# and its question row opens the answer deck over the desktop
# (both are layer-shell, not clients).
.RE
.fi
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/monowl/notifications/
Queue directory (or
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/monowl/notifications/
when set):
.B .txt
plain messages,
.B .q
questions,
.B .answer
answer rendezvous files (never listed).
.TP
.I notification-gui
Answer deck binary, looked up beside the running monopanel binary
(repo: panel/notification-gui). Accepts
.B --help
and
.B --dry-run
(list queued questions plus answers without needing a display).
With
.B --preview
it opens the tiny preview popup (up to 3 oldest plain rows + 1
question row that routes to the deck);
.B --preview --dry-run
prints the preview rows without needing a display.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR monopanel (1)
